The Managing Director/Chief Executive Officer of Fina Trust Microfinance Bank, Bevan Pereira, has called on Nigerians to cultivate a consistent savings culture, highlighting the importance of financial discipline, starting small and building savings gradually, irrespective of income level.
Speaking on the theme, “Building a Consistent Savings Culture in Nigeria,” in commemoration of World Savings Day, he noted that Nigeria already has a longstanding tradition of savings, rooted in cooperative societies, family practices and community-based contribution schemes, but stressed the need to strengthen this culture through deliberate and consistent financial habits.
&nbsp;
According to him, savings should not be viewed merely as whatever remains after spending, but as a deliberate decision to set aside a portion of one’s income before meeting other expenses.
&nbsp;
He said that while saving can be challenging, particularly under difficult economic conditions, developing the habit requires personal discipline and a willingness to begin with whatever amount is affordable.
&nbsp;
“Saving is not a sprint; it is a marathon. It is about what you build over a period of time. It is not about how much you start with, but where you start. Whether it is N500 or another amount you can afford, the important thing is to develop the habit and remain consistent,” he said.
&nbsp;
Pereira also cautioned against adopting rigid savings formulas without considering individual financial situations, noting that the amount and approach to saving should reflect one’s income, responsibilities and prevailing circumstances.
&nbsp;
He encouraged Nigerians, particularly young people, to focus on making a deliberate start rather than postponing savings until they can afford to set aside substantial amounts.
&nbsp;
He said that the ability to see and measure progress can encourage younger generations to remain engaged with their savings goals.
&nbsp;
“What matters is making a deliberate start, no matter how small the amount. You do not have to wait until you have a substantial sum before you begin saving. As you consistently put money aside and see your savings grow, that progress can motivate you to stay committed to your financial goals, particularly among young people,” he added.

The African Development Bank Group (AfDB) and Agence Française de Développement (AFD) have signed a new memorandum of understanding (MoU) for 2026–2031, building on a partnership that has supported 17 jointly financed projects across 14 African countries since 2021.
A statement published on the AfDB website said the agreement was signed on November 10 on the sidelines of the Paris Peace Forum.
The pact seeks to strengthen cooperation from the early stages of project development through financing and implementation, with both institutions aiming to expand the pipeline of investment-ready projects across the continent.
Under the agreement, the institutions will jointly assess prospective projects and combine financing with project preparation, technical advice, policy dialogue and capacity building.
&nbsp;
They will also deepen collaboration on project management, monitoring and knowledge transfer.
&nbsp;
The partnership will focus on several priority areas on energy transition infrastructure, transport, water and sanitation, and regional connectivity.
&nbsp;
It also focuses on agriculture, industry, private sector development and entrepreneurship;
access to finance and job creation, particularly for women and young people.
&nbsp;
Others include data governance and the responsible use of artificial intelligence as well as climate resilience, biodiversity, health, education and governance.
&nbsp;
AfDB President Dr Sidi Ould Tah said the partnership would help direct capital towards infrastructure, stronger African value chains and employment opportunities for young people.
&nbsp;
“The Paris Peace Forum is a fitting setting to renew our cooperation with AFD. Peace and development are built together, and delivering on Africa’s priorities requires close collaboration,” he said.
AFD Group Chief Executive Officer Christophe Lecourtier described the agreement as a new step in the institutions’ relationship, noting that the partnership would support strategic infrastructure, particularly in energy and transport, as well as industrial development and long-term economic growth.
&nbsp;
The agreement also provides for greater cooperation on blended finance, guarantees, catalytic finance and carbon finance to attract additional public and private investment.
&nbsp;
The institutions will explore access to European financial instruments and co-financing platforms involving European institutions and African partners.
&nbsp;
The partnership builds on a relationship first formalised in 2008 and renewed in 2021.
&nbsp;
France joined the African Development Fund, the Bank Group’s concessional financing window, in 1977 and the African Development Bank in 1983.
&nbsp;
Energy has accounted for nearly half of the institutions’ joint financing since 2021.
&nbsp;
AFD has also committed €1 billion to Mission 300, an initiative of the AfDB and the World Bank aimed at connecting an additional 300 million people in sub-Saharan Africa to electricity by 2030.

