items.content:
BAIC FOTON has strengthened its African expansion strategy through new commercial vehicle deliveries, product launches and local manufacturing agreements at its 2026 Africa Partners Conference in Port Elizabeth, South Africa.
&nbsp;
The conference, themed “Drive to Great”, attracted nearly 700 participants, including government and business representatives, company executives, distributors, industrial partners and media organisations from 42 African countries.
&nbsp;
Held on October 29 at Nelson Mandela Bay Stadium, the event focused on expanding local manufacturing, strengthening partnerships and developing the company’s presence across the continent.
&nbsp;
At the conference, BAIC Group CEO and BAIC FOTON Chairman, Chang Rui, joined customer representatives to mark the delivery of 1,000 heavy-duty trucks in Ghana and 800 in Zambia.
&nbsp;
The company also signed new energy vehicle project agreements covering 200 units in Mozambique, 100 each in Ethiopia and Kenya, and a batch order in Zambia. The projects are expected to support green transition initiatives in sectors including mining and agriculture.
&nbsp;
Speaking on the company’s strategy, BAIC FOTON International President, Wu Zongtian, identified localised product offerings, business ecosystems, manufacturing and operations as the three pillars of its African expansion.
&nbsp;
He said the company would deploy different technologies, including fuel-powered, natural gas, battery-electric and plug-in hybrid vehicles, to meet varying transport and energy needs across African markets.
&nbsp;
The company also unveiled two heavy-duty trucks, GALAXUS R5 and GALAXUS C5, while introducing the TUNLAND V9 plug-in hybrid pickup to the African market.
&nbsp;
The trucks were developed for demanding operating conditions, including long-distance transportation and construction, while the hybrid pickup combines fuel and electric power to reduce energy consumption.
&nbsp;
BAIC FOTON also signed six local manufacturing cooperation agreements with partners in Tanzania, Kenya and Zimbabwe. It further advanced vehicle purchase support cooperation with WesBank and an African spare parts centre warehouse partnership with DP World.
&nbsp;
The company said the agreements would extend its relationships with African partners beyond vehicle sales to manufacturing, financing and after-sales support. It currently operates across 54 African markets, with more than 300 authorised service outlets and 16 localised factories.
&nbsp;
The conference also recognised partners for contributions to customer development, new energy promotion, after-sales services and distribution.
&nbsp;
In addition, the company launched the African leg of its GALAXUS Global Experience programme, with distributors from Tanzania, the Democratic Republic of Congo, Zambia and Zimbabwe receiving flags to coordinate activities in their markets.
&nbsp;
BAIC FOTON said it would continue expanding local manufacturing, strengthening supply networks and improving customer services to support transportation and industrial development across Africa.

