items.content:
Amnesty International Nigeria has called for urgent action to address persistent oil pollution in the Niger Delta, urging the Federal Government to assess its impact on public health, water resources, fisheries and agricultural livelihoods.
&nbsp;
The organisation also called for a comprehensive environmental restoration programme across the region, warning that ongoing cleanup efforts in Ogoniland should not be regarded as a solution to pollution affecting other communities.
&nbsp;
Speaking at the launch of its report in Port Harcourt, Amnesty International’s Campaign Network Coordinator for Business and Human Rights, Dominic Twomey, said the findings were based on documentary evidence, internal emails, confidential audits and court documents, including materials from legal proceedings involving Bille and Ogale communities in Rivers State.
&nbsp;
The report, titled Nigeria: Lifting the Lid, was published in collaboration with human rights and environmental organisations.
&nbsp;
It examines the historical environmental impact of Shell’s operations in the Niger Delta before the divestment of its onshore subsidiary.
&nbsp;
According to Amnesty, documents disclosed during legal proceedings in the United Kingdom provide further insight into the management and maintenance of the company’s former Nigerian operations.
&nbsp;
The organisation said the findings reinforced longstanding concerns raised by Niger Delta communities and environmental justice groups, highlighting the need for closer attention to oil infrastructure maintenance, pipeline repairs and recurring spills.
&nbsp;
It called for independent assessments of operational and decommissioned oil facilities, with findings made publicly available to promote transparency and accountability.
&nbsp;
Amnesty also recommended a well-funded environmental restoration fund to support cleanup across the Niger Delta to internationally accepted standards. It urged the United Kingdom and Netherlands to strengthen requirements for multinational companies to conduct human rights and environmental assessments of their global operations.
&nbsp;
The Director of Amnesty International Nigeria, Isa Sanusi, said the organisation would engage the Federal Government to ensure its recommendations received attention.
&nbsp;
He said the Ogoniland cleanup should not substitute for addressing pollution across the wider region.
&nbsp;
“We are also calling on all fossil fuel companies operating in Nigeria, including Shell, to contribute to a super fund with sufficient resources to fully clean up the Niger Delta to international standards,” Sanusi said.
&nbsp;
He added that the organisation would write to President Bola Tinubu and submit the report for consideration.
&nbsp;
A campaigner with ReCommon, Eva Pastorelli, said the report provided documentary evidence for further examination of environmental responsibilities associated with Shell’s historical operations.
&nbsp;
Meanwhile, Team Lead of the Media and Environmental Development Initiative, Dr Celestine Akpobari, urged sustained efforts to restore polluted waterways and farmlands, enabling residents to return to fishing, farming and other traditional livelihoods.

